    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a> began not in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> but in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a>, ‘the One-Eyed’, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s senior officers, had governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> since 333 BCE, and in the wars of the Successors after 323 BCE he built up the largest of the competing realms, stretching from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. In 307 BCE his son Demetrius expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>'s governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-phalerum">Demetrius of Phalerum</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and in 306 BCE, after Demetrius destroyed Ptolemy's fleet off Salamis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, Antigonus took the title of king and conferred it on his son, the first of the Successors to do so. Their bid for Alexander's whole empire failed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE, where Antigonus was killed. Demetrius, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Poliorcetes</a>, ‘the Besieger’, kept his fleet and several Greek cities, and in 294 BCE had the young Alexander V murdered and made himself king of Macedon, only to be driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> in 288 BCE. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a> held on to a string of Greek fortresses, and after destroying a Gallic army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a> in 277 BCE he won the Macedonian throne, which his family kept until 168 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thessaloniki.jpg" alt="Thessaloniki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tessaloniki_BW_2017-10-05_18-22-47.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Berthold Werner, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was a land of farmers, herders and a powerful landed aristocracy. The lowland plains around Pella and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliacmon">Haliacmon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axius</a> rivers produced grain, the uplands supported flocks, and the forests supplied ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and pitch, a royal resource coveted by every Hellenistic navy. The kings controlled the mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pangaion">Pangaeum</a> region and other districts, and Philip V in particular reopened mines, raised revenues and resettled populations to rebuild the kingdom's manpower after its wars. Macedonian society was less urban than that of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, though old and new cities flourished: Pella was the royal seat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> and Cassandreia were major ports, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> around 293 BCE on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasetic-gulf">Gulf of Pagasae</a>, became a royal residence and fortress. The court attracted intellectuals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus Gonatas</a> was a student and friend of the Stoic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium">Zeno of Citium</a>, whose pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persaeus">Persaeus</a> lived at his court; the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-soli">Aratus of Soli</a> wrote his astronomical poem, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaenomena">Phaenomena</a></em>, there; and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieronymus-of-cardia">Hieronymus of Cardia</a>, who served three generations of the dynasty, wrote the history of the Successors on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> later drew. Religion followed the traditional Greek and Macedonian cults, and the kings advertised their piety with dedications and buildings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> and other sanctuaries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia.jpg" alt="Battle of Sellasia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Battle of Sellasia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SouthernPeloponessus.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Semperf, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid</a> power was the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, a dense formation of infantry armed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a>, a pike some five to six metres long, supported by peltasts, cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The kingdom usually fielded armies of 20,000 to 30,000 men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> raised around 40,000 for the final war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The early Antigonids were also great naval powers: Demetrius won the sea battle off Salamis in 306 BCE, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 305–304 BCE with enormous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a>, and built some of the largest warships of the age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus Gonatas</a> defeated the Ptolemaic fleet off Cos, at a date still debated, and the Chremonidean War (c. 267–261 BCE) ended with the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Macedonian garrisons in its harbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Battle of Sellasia</a> in 222 BCE. Against Rome the phalanx proved inflexible on broken ground: Philip V was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus">Titus Quinctius Flamininus</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> in 197 BCE, and Perseus' army was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Lucius Aemilius Paullus</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Battle of Pydna</a> in 168 BCE, when Roman maniples exploited gaps in the advancing line. Garrisons at Corinth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a>, which Philip V reportedly called the ‘fetters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>’, anchored Macedonian control of the south.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson.jpg" alt="Antigonus III Doson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antigonus_Doson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">uploader - Bjankuloski06, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid</a> king ruled as a personal monarch, commanding the army, conducting diplomacy and controlling much of the land and its resources. He governed with a circle of ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a>’ (<em>philoi</em>), courtiers who served as generals, envoys and governors, and he relied on the loyalty of the Macedonian nobility and the citizen army. Dedications made in the name of ‘King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a>’ show that the Macedonians kept a collective identity alongside the crown. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemies</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, the Antigonids ruled a largely Macedonian population and did not promote a royal cult at home. Royal letters and ordinances regulated the cities, which kept their own magistrates, sometimes under royal overseers (<em>epistatai</em>), and an inscription from Amphipolis preserves part of Philip V's regulations for the army. In southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the kings worked through garrisons, friendly tyrants and, from 224 BCE, the Hellenic alliance that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus Doson</a> led against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. Succession was usually orderly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Gonatas</a> was followed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-aetolicus">Demetrius II</a> (239–229 BCE), and when Demetrius died leaving the boy Philip V, his cousin Antigonus Doson ruled first as guardian and then as king before passing the throne to Philip in 221 BCE. Gonatas reportedly told his son that kingship was a ‘glorious servitude’, a phrase that captures the dynasty's sober style of rule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/athens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="Athens" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Parlette, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For over a century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonids</a> were the dominant power in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and the politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and the Aetolian and Achaean leagues revolved around their garrisons and alliances. They gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> a long period of stable government after the chaos of the Successor wars and the Gallic invasion, and their court was a centre of Stoic philosophy, Hellenistic poetry and historical writing. Their story also shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s entry into the Greek East. Philip V's treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in 215 BCE drew Rome across the Adriatic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war">First Macedonian War</a> (214–205 BCE), and the Second and Third Macedonian Wars turned Rome into the arbiter of Greece. In 196 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus">Flamininus</a> proclaimed the freedom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmian-games">Isthmian Games</a>, a policy that soon gave way to direct control. The fall of Macedon brought Greek culture to Rome on a new scale: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Aemilius Paullus</a> let his sons take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a>' library, and among the thousand Achaean hostages deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, whose account of Rome's rise centres on the Macedonian wars. Through Polybius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>'s lives of Demetrius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a> and Aemilius Paullus, the dynasty became for later readers a classic example of Hellenistic kingship and of its defeat by Rome.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae.jpg" alt="Battle of Cynoscephalae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kryston, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid</a> decline was driven by the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Philip V (221–179 BCE) began energetically, winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-220-217-bce">Social War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> (220–217 BCE), but his alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> made Rome an enemy, and his aggressive campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> after 202 BCE alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, which appealed to the Senate. Defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Cynoscephalae</a> in 197 BCE cost him his possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, most of his fleet and an indemnity of 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, and confined him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. He cooperated with Rome against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> and rebuilt his kingdom's finances and manpower, but his reign ended in bitterness after he had his younger son Demetrius, a favourite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, executed in 180 BCE. His elder son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> (179–168 BCE) restored Macedonian prestige among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, which Rome's ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II of Pergamon</a> denounced before the Senate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> broke out in 171 BCE; after early successes Perseus was decisively beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Pydna</a> in June 168 BCE. He surrendered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a>, walked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Paullus</a>' triumph in Rome and died in captivity at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-fucens">Alba Fucens</a>. In 167 BCE Rome abolished the monarchy and split the kingdom into four republics, and after the revolt of the pretender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriscus">Andriscus</a>, Macedonia became a Roman province in the 140s BCE.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia, founded by Alexander's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> I and his heirs, dominated mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> for more than a century with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and fortresses, patronised Stoic philosophy and Hellenistic letters, and fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Pydna</a> in 168 BCE, opening the Greek world to Roman rule.</em></p>

